-- double_exponential_smoothing(m[3m], 0.5, 0.5): drops the metric name; host4 has one sample and is dropped.
-- host1 (10,20,30) -> 30, host2 (25,15,5) -> 5, host3 (10,20,15) -> 22.5.
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	30
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	5
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	22.5
-- double_exponential_smoothing(m[3m], 0.8, 0.3): different factors.
-- host1 -> 30, host2 -> 5, host3 (10,20,15) -> 18.
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	30
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	5
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	18
-- Invalid factors are rejected (must be in the open interval (0, 1)).
expects smoothing factor in the open interval (0, 1)
expects trend factor in the open interval (0, 1)
-- The factors may be scalar expressions, like in Prometheus: the same results as with the constant factors above.
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	30
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	5
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	22.5
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	30
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	5
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	18
-- Computed factors out of range are rejected too.
expects smoothing factor in the open interval (0, 1)
expects trend factor in the open interval (0, 1)
-- A NaN factor is not an error, like in Prometheus. The result is NaN, but the trend factor is unused for two samples.
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	nan
[('instance','host1')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	30
[('instance','host2')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	5
[('instance','host3')]	2023-11-14 22:13:20.000	15
-- Like in Prometheus, the factors are checked only where a window has samples, so a selector without series is not an error.
-- The factors are read at every step: sf = 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.5 and tf = 0.8, 0.7, 0.6, 0.5 (Prometheus: 7.8, 2, 11.4, 6.5).
[('instance','host5')]	[(1699999940,7.8000000000000025),(1699999960,1.9999999999999996),(1699999980,11.4),(1700000000,6.5)]
-- sf = 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1: the last step has samples, so the query fails.
expects smoothing factor in the open interval (0, 1), got 1
-- sf = 0.5, 1.5, 2.5: the windows of the last two steps have no samples, so it is not an error.
[('instance','host5')]	[(1700000000,6.5)]
-- A fixed @ works with a factor that is the same at every step, but not with a factor that varies per step.
[('instance','host1')]	30
[('instance','host2')]	5
[('instance','host3')]	22.5
NOT_IMPLEMENTED
-- The subquery window (1699999990, 1700000000] contains no 1m step, so the result is empty (and not an error about the factors).
-- An empty range vector gives an empty result before the factors are checked, as in Prometheus.
-- A Float32 series gives the same Float64 result as a Float64 series: the result is not rounded to Float32.
1	Array(Nullable(Float64))	1
